- Groq failures from the user's Agy session:
  - qwen/qwen3.8-27b: HTTP 413 rate_limit_exceeded, ITPM limit 7,000, request input 36,430 tokens.
  - openai/gpt-oss-120b: HTTP 413 rate_limit_exceeded, TPM limit 8,000, request 24,890 tokens.
- These error values are actual account/on-demand quota feedback. Groq applies organization-level limits and may expose both combined TPM and separate ITPM/OTPM. Do not blindly assign a generic public/free-tier TPM value to the current single groqTPM setting.

### Confirmed Agy implementation findings on work

- internal/agent/agent.go: RunContext refreshes configured MCP servers, then builds a model request on every generation cycle from the complete a.messages history and declarationsAsTools().
- declarationsAsTools() appends every tool returned by a.mcp.Tools() as a model-visible function declaration when MCP tools are allowed. The connected GitHub server has 91 tools and Git server has 14, giving 105 MCP declarations plus built-in tools.
- internal/openaicompat/client.go: buildProviderRequest serializes every item in request.Contents into chat messages. chatTools() flattens all function declarations into the OpenAI-compatible tools array. Therefore the entire conversation history and every discovered MCP schema are serialized again on each model turn.
- This is a verified source of avoidable request growth. Do not claim the 105 schemas alone account for the precise 24,890 or 36,430 tokens: no raw request/tokenizer breakdown has been captured yet. Instrument per-section sizes and compare a fresh session with/without MCP to quantify.
- internal/openaicompat/client.go: Groq response parsing in readStream() accumulates content and tool calls but does not capture usage fields. decodeCompletion() also parses choices/tool calls only. The request currently does not have a verified streamed usage collection path. Check current Groq stream_options semantics before implementing.
- internal/gemini/client.go defines Response.Usage, but Generate() returns Content only, discarding usage. internal/gemini/stream.go parses SSE candidates/parts but does not retain usageMetadata. The modelClient interface in internal/agent/agent.go returns only Content/error, so there is no provider-neutral per-turn usage result to aggregate.
- internal/openaicompat/nvidia_rate_limit.go estimates token count as serialized JSON bytes divided by four plus reserved output tokens. This is a heuristic, not Groq's tokenizer or actual metering.
- internal/config/config.go defaults GroqRPM=0 and GroqTPM=0, disabling local RPM/TPM pacing. There is one Groq TPM field, not separate ITPM and OTPM budgets. The current estimate also combines approximate input and reserved output into a single total. This cannot accurately model the user's split ITPM quota.
- maxTurns=500 is a count of generation/tool cycles, not a context or token budget.

### External references checked on 2026-11-17

1. Aichat (Rust, open source)
- config.example.yaml enables function_calling, supports mapping_tools and use_tools, has compress_threshold=4000, and has a right prompt capable of showing consume_tokens and percentage.
- Its FAQ explains that stateless chat APIs receive the full history each request; growing history increases transmitted/processed data and can exceed model limits. Aichat automatically summarizes session history when its token threshold is reached.
- User's example uses one generic execute_command shell function, unlike Agy's 105 individual GitHub/Git MCP schemas. Compare equivalent fresh-session scenarios.
- Sources: https://github.com/sigoden/aichat/blob/main/config.example.yaml ; https://github.com/sigoden/aichat/wiki/FAQ ; https://github.com/sigoden/aichat/wiki/Configuration-Guide

2. Google Gemini CLI (open source)
- MCP settings support includeTools allowlists and excludeTools blocklists. By default all discovered tools are enabled. This is a useful explicit filter but not lazy discovery by itself.
- Compression is token-threshold-driven (default threshold is 50 percent of model token limit); preserves the latest three tool-response turns in full and collapses/truncates older large tool responses selectively. Retrieval tools such as read_file, grep, rip_grep, and MCP resource reads receive exemptions from some collapsing.
- Context telemetry breaks estimated context into system instructions, tool definitions, history, tool calls, and MCP definitions/results rather than one opaque prompt total.
- Text token counts use local estimation; media can use the model CountTokens API.
- Sources: https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/docs/tools/mcp-server.md ; https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/packages/core/src/context/chatCompressionService.ts ; https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/packages/core/src/core/loggingContentGenerator.ts ; https://github.com/google-gemini/gemini-cli/blob/main/packages/core/src/utils/tokenCalculation.ts

3. OpenAI Codex CLI (Rust, open source)
- Context accounting distinguishes active context tokens, auto-compaction-scope tokens, model context-window limit, and remaining capacity. Compaction is driven by token thresholds rather than turn count.
- OpenAI's agent-loop writeup explains compacting the old conversation into a smaller representation; the Responses API compact endpoint returns a compaction item that preserves additional latent state.
- Codex usage UI tracks input tokens, cached input tokens, cache-write input tokens, output tokens, reasoning output tokens, and total tokens. It retains latest response and accumulated session usage.
- Sources: https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/core/src/session/context_window.rs ; https://github.com/openai/codex/blob/main/codex-rs/tui/src/token_usage.rs ; https://openai.com/index/unrolling-the-codex-agent-loop/

4. Anthropic Claude tool-use architecture (Claude Code itself is not open source; use the official platform/API documentation rather than claiming its CLI implementation is inspectable)
- Tool search/deferred loading keeps most tool definitions out of the initial context and exposes definitions on demand. Anthropic's published example compares 50+ tools costing about 72K upfront schema tokens against a small search tool plus 3–5 relevant definitions, reporting an 85 percent reduction in that example.
- Programmatic tool calling can execute sequences of tool operations as code, avoiding multiple inference roundtrips and keeping intermediate results out of the normal conversation transcript.
- Prompt caching reduces repeated input billing for stable tool definitions but does not shrink context usage. Context editing removes stale tool results.
- Sources: https://platform.claude.com/docs/en/agents-and-tools/tool-use/manage-tool-context ; https://www.anthropic.com/research/advanced-tool-use

5. OpenCode (open source)
- Automatic compaction summarizes older history while retaining a recent tail (docs describe about 15K tokens by default); optional pruning removes older tool outputs.
- There have been upstream MCP registration persistence issues around compaction. Agy must keep/rebuild the connected tool registry through compaction and test this invariant.
- Sources: https://opencode.ai/docs/compaction/ ; https://opencode.ai/docs/config/

6. ChatGPT connected apps/plugins
- User uses ChatGPT with GitHub connected and considers selective connected-app tool use the UX reference. Treat it as a behavior/UX benchmark only; do not infer private internal implementation details.

